          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    
    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        Fading post-acne dark spots requires calming active inflammation, interrupting excess melanin transfer, and shielding healing skin with daily sunscreen.
    </p>

    
    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            Dark spots left behind by acne fade as your skin sheds pigmented surface cells, but the process usually takes eight to twelve weeks of consistent daily care. To speed up fading, use gentle pigment inhibitors like kojic acid and niacinamide alongside daily broad-spectrum sunscreen, and never pick or squeeze blemishes. If you are still getting active breakouts, treat the acne first, because every fresh pimple starts the pigmentation cycle over again.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When a pimple clears, it often leaves a flat brown or dark mark behind. In skincare, this is called post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation. It is not an indented scar, but extra pigment the skin makes while healing from swelling and irritation. While the bump itself is gone, the dark mark can linger for months, making your skin look uneven long after the acne has settled.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        This happens especially easily on Indian skin tones. Skin with more natural melanin has pigment cells that react strongly to any irritation. Whether a breakout was a deep, painful bump or a tiny blemish picked in front of a mirror, the skin rushes melanin to the area to protect itself. Trying to scrub these marks away with harsh exfoliants only creates fresh irritation, making the spots darker and harder to fade.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Why post-acne dark marks form on the skin
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When a pore clogs with oil and dead skin, inflammation signals nearby pigment cells to produce excess melanin. In deeper skin tones, such as Fitzpatrick skin types IV to VI, these cells react strongly and produce pigment quickly.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20725554/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">[1]</a></sup>
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        It helps to distinguish between the three marks a breakout can leave behind:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation (PIH).</strong> Flat brown or dark marks caused by extra melanin. Common in medium to deep skin tones, these respond well to topical skincare.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Post-inflammatory erythema (PIE).</strong> Pink or red marks caused by dilated microscopic blood vessels near the surface, more common in fair skin.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Acne scars.</strong> Pitted indentations or raised tissue caused by collagen loss or damage, which topical creams cannot fill.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Topical serums work well because epidermal pigment clears as skin cells naturally turn over.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21348540/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">[2]</a></sup> When a blemish was picked or deeply inflamed, pigment can drop into the dermis, where cells do not shed monthly. Those deeper marks take much longer to clear.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        First priority: stop picking and control active acne
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Before buying brightening serums, address two habits that prolong dark spots: picking blemishes and ignoring fresh breakouts. Squeezing a pimple tears skin and drives inflammation deeper, turning a minor breakout into a stubborn mark.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        You also cannot fade marks if you break out every week. Each new pimple triggers another wave of pigment, restarting the clock on your skin. Dermatologists emphasize that controlling active breakouts is the necessary first step before or alongside treating dark marks.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42234395/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">[3]</a></sup> If you still have active acne, use a gentle formula with salicylic acid to clear pores and Centella asiatica to calm redness. Once breakouts settle, your skin can focus on clearing older pigment.
    </p>

    
    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
